[0.000, "o", "d hardware initialization (QSPI, SDRAM, audio codec, LEDs,\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37metc.) automatically. The next step involves examining the audio callback implementations located in the relevant\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mheader and source files to understand the audio data handling mechanisms.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/src/hid/audio.h)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mRead 166 lines\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/src/hid/audio.cpp)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mRead 60 lines\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/src/per/adc.h)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mRead 180 lines\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/src/per/dac.h)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mRead 130 lines\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m▾ Thought for 11s, 1.0k tokens\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The system's external devices reside in the `src/dev/` directory. These include audio codecs such as Ak4556,\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mWm8731, and Pcm3060, various memory chips like Sdram and flash memories (IS25LP064A, IS25LP0"]
[0.000, "o", "80D), and shift\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mregisters/port expanders such as ShiftRegister4021 and ShiftRegister595.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The `src/dev/` directory also contains drivers for various display technologies like OLED and LCD, along with\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mdrivers for LEDs (e.g., Neopixels), and sensors such as capacitive touch, gesture/color, barometric pressure, 3D\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mmagnetic, and IMU sensors. libDaisy provides the hardware abstraction and board support for the Daisy platform,\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mwhile DaisySP provides the DSP functionality. The DSP code is executed within the audio callback. Development uses\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37man ARM toolchain with `make` or `cmake`, and flashing happens via DFU, JTAG, or the Daisy bootloader.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/tests/CMakeLists.txt)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mRead 80 lines\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/doc/md/_b1_Development-Unit-Testing.md)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  "]
[0.000, "o", "⎿  \u001b[mRead 99 lines\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mBash\u001b[m\u001b[37m(git status; git log -n 5 --oneline)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mOn branch master\u001b[K\r\n            Your branch is up to date with 'origin/master'.\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\r\n            nothing to commit, working tree clean\u001b[K\r\n            f715e48 (grafted, HEAD -> master, origin/master, origin/HEAD) update README w/ daisyaudio org change, and\u001b[K\r\n            set build badge to use master branch (#722)\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/src/version.h)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mRead 10 lines\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mRead\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/tmp/workspace/libDaisy/CHANGELOG.md)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mRead 100 lines\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m▾ Thought for 1m 28s, 3.3k tokens\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  Initial assessment indicates a range of hardware platforms and development kits supported. The \"libDaisy\" core\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37marchitecture appears central to this ecosystem.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The system appears to be structured around modular component"]
[0.000, "o", "s, starting from a System-on-Module (SOM) interface\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mand expanding into peripheral drivers and external device support. This suggests a focus on hardware abstraction\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mand reusability across different DaisySeed hardware targets. The inclusion of human interface device (HID)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mcomponents, a UI system, and utility helpers points toward a complete embedded development ecosystem for audio\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mapplications.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The STM32H750IB's memory map dictates several crucial placement rules for DMA buffers and large audio data to\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mensure real-time performance. DMA buffers must reside in the non-cached SRAM_D2 region, while large audio buffers\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mshould be allocated in the SDRAM and initialized after hardware initialization. The audio callback's execution time\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mis constrained by the block duration within the interrupt service routine, which directly impacts the chosen "]
[0.000, "o", "memory\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mand buffer configurations.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The audio callback design adheres to strict real-time constraints, disallowing memory allocation, blocking calls,\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mand extensive logging to maintain performance. Data transfer between the callback and the main loop must use lock-\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mfree data structures to prevent race conditions. The project setup offers both Makefile and CMake build systems,\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37malong with various flashing and debugging methods. There are instructions on how to set up logging to view the\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37moutput using the USB CDC. It is important to note that artifacts are used to present structured information to the\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37muser.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The developer guide's structure should begin with an executive overview, detailing the purpose of `libDaisy`,\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37msupported hardware, and core subsystem layering.  It needs clear Mark"]
[0.000, "o", "down formatting and GitHub-style links for\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37measy navigation. The `RequestFeedback` metadata field should be set to true.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  I am considering including a section on real-time constraints and data transfer methods due to their importance,\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mbut I'll need to see how the existing documentation covers them. I'm also planning to incorporate sections on build\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37msystems and debugging.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The artifact is located at: [/Users/brett/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/a59186a1-d894-46cf-95a9-\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mab842675eb98/libdaisy_developer_guide.md](file:///Users/brett/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/a59186a1-d894-46cf-\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m95a9-ab842675eb98/libdaisy_developer_guide.md).\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  Do you want me to start building out the outline?\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  The system architecture involves low-level MCU features and external peripherals. The documentation cove"]
[0.000, "o", "rs\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mhardware abstractions, human interfaces, UI, and utility functions. Development tools encompass both Makefile and\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mCMake build systems, along with different flashing options.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  Critical memory configurations, including SDRAM, SRAM, and DTCM, are detailed, showing how memory section\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mplacement affects DMA buffer usage, cache coherency, and overall performance. Key aspects of the audio engine and\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mDSP programming, such as interleaved/non-interleaved buffers, sample rates, and block sizes, are highlighted.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  Audio ISR programming relies on \"Golden Rules\" to ensure real-time performance. These rules include prohibiting\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mmemory allocation, blocking calls, and extensive logging, while enforcing lock-free communication. CPU load\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mmonitoring and integration with a DSP library are essential elements.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "     \u001b[37m  Peripheral and hardware interface examples will be incorporated to illustrate common use cases.  This includes\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mrecipes for GPIO, analog control, MIDI processing, serial logging, persistent storage, and displays.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  \u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37m  Board support packages and hardware pin mappings are important and should be documented.  Unit testing and\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n     \u001b[37mhardware isolation techniques will be covered as part of the quality assurance strategy.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[32m●\u001b[m \u001b[1;33mEdit\u001b[m\u001b[37m(~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/a59186a1-...-ab842675eb98/libdaisy_developer_guide.md)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  ⎿  \u001b[mWrote 632 lines \u001b[37m(ctrl+o to collapse)\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m▾ Thought for 7s, 335 tokens\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n\u001b[37m  The developer guide has been created. The primary goal is to address the user's request and adhere to the\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n  \u001b[37martifact rule. The user will be pointed to the newly generated guide.\u001b[m\u001b[K\r\n  I have reviewed \u001b]8;id=jk8b2e3333e6e47474;file:///Users/brett/tmp/workspace/libD"]
